The epsitemic attitude of the scientific realism prevalent in the modern sciences appears to be one of the disastrous avenue for the scientific progress in the modern age. It is appropriate to consider a refined definition of scientific realism, with a limited consensus, though there could be as many understandings of realism as the number of practioners of sciences. The most common formulation of scientific realism is following:

> "Scientific realism is a positive epistemic attitude toward the content of our best theories and models, recommending belief in both observable and unobservable aspects of the world described by the sciences [1]."

